  "text": "**Solution 2 (answer 3) is wrong.** Its own counterexamples show that the property fails at n = 5. The n = 5 construction is valid: take the single 4-edge {1,2,3,4} and all 3-edges containing vertex 5. Every triple is covered, but any 5-cycle of vertices has two consecutive triples avoiding vertex 5. Both of those triples lie inside {1,2,3,4}, so both must be covered by the one 4-edge, and no Berge-C₅³ exists. So n₀ ≥ 6, and the answer cannot be 3.\n\n**Solution 1 (answer 7) is not established.** The n = 4 and n = 5 counterexamples are correct, so n₀ ≥ 6 is right. The rest of the argument does not hold up:\n\n- **n = 6:** The claimed counterexample is asserted without checking. The natural candidates I tested all contain the required cycles. The six 4-edges that cover all triples (complements forming two disjoint triangles) admit Berge-C₄³, C₅³ and C₆³. Replacing the 4-edges with 3-edges only makes cycles easier to find.\n- **n = 7:** The argument rests on a Steiner system S(3,4,7), which is irrelevant to whether the property holds. The claim that larger n \"inevitably\" works is not proved.\n\nSo 7 may be the true value, but nothing here establishes it. I could not verify the answer, and neither solution's final answer is supported by a valid argument.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
